md`# SoundScript

SoundScript is TypeScript with things taken out, for writing notebook cells. A checker reads each cell and accepts it only if everything the cell depends on is in its parameter list. For an accepted cell the notebook knows what can change its value, so it can skip it when nothing changed, run it on another thread, and replay it in a test.

This page is the tutorial. Every example is a live cell of this notebook, except the boxes of mistakes in sections 2 and 8: open its editor, change it, and press Shift-Enter. The first, \`width\`, is under the next heading.`
// %%
md`## A first cell

\`width\`, \`height\` and \`area\` are three cells. A cell is a function. Its parameter names are the cells it reads, so \`area\` runs again when \`width\` or \`height\` changes.

The listing before them is their source, read back from the cells as they are now. With edit mode on, each cell's value follows the listing, and the **edit** bar under a cell opens its editor.`
// %%
sourceListing(tutorial_module, invalidation, { from: "width", to: "area" })
// %%
//ss: 01-cells.ts -aspect -scale -half -test_half
// %%
md`**Try it.** Open \`width\` and change it to \`() => 800\`, then press Shift-Enter. \`area\`, \`aspect\` and \`half\` update. Now change it to \`() => "800"\`. The edit is stored, and the editor says which cells it breaks: \`area\` and \`aspect\` do arithmetic on \`width\`, so they no longer check. They show the checker's message and stop running. \`half\` reads \`area\`, so it is blocked: it shows the error \`area\` raises, and it does not run until \`area\` does. Change \`width\` back, or change those two cells to use \`Number(width)\`, and they run again.`
// %%
md`## Why take things out

An ordinary notebook cell can do anything JavaScript can. It can read \`document\`, call \`Math.random\`, or change an object that another cell returned. The runtime cannot see any of that, so it has to assume the worst: run every downstream cell again on every change, never reuse a result, and keep everything on the main thread.

In the lopecode notebooks this is common. Of 8,650 cells counted on 2026-11-08 (279 modules, one declared canonical copy of each, from the lopecode and lopebooks notebook collections, each cell parsed and its free names compared with its inputs), 941 read a browser global that is not one of their inputs; \`document\` appears in 347 cells and \`window\` in 291.

SoundScript removes the constructs that hide a dependency or an effect. What is left is still TypeScript, and an editor highlights it as TypeScript.`
// %%
md`## The four rules

1. **Inputs are parameters.** A cell body may use its parameters, its own locals and a fixed list of built-ins. There are no globals.
2. **Effects are parameters.** The clock, randomness, the network and logging are *capabilities*: reserved parameter names (\`now\`, \`random\`, \`fetch\`, \`log\`) that the notebook supplies. A cell that can give two answers for the same inputs says so in its signature.
3. **Values do not change after they are returned.** A cell may build a value with local writes; once returned, it is read-only everywhere.
4. **State changes only in actions.** State is a state cell, made by \`state(...)\`. A write to it must be inside \`action(...)\`, and a cell never calls an action. The page calls it, when a button is clicked or a frame arrives.

Sections 1 to 5 teach these rules. Sections 6 to 9 show what they pay for.`
// %%
md`## 1. Cells and their inputs

The other cells of the file that \`width\`, \`height\` and \`area\` came from. A parameter may leave out its type; it then has the type of the cell it names. A cell whose value is a function is written as two arrows, as \`scale\` is. A cell named \`test_…\` is a test: it passes if it does not throw.

The diagram after them shows which cells each one reads.`
// %%
sourceListing(tutorial_module, invalidation, { from: "aspect", to: "test_half" })
// %%
//ss: 01-cells.ts -width -height -area
// %%
depGraph(tutorial_module, invalidation, { only: ["width", "height", "area", "aspect", "scale", "half", "test_half"] })
// %%
md`## 2. What the checker rejects

Eight cells: five each break one rule, \`width\` and \`label\` are the cells they read, and \`jittered\` is \`jitter\` written correctly. They are shown in a box and checked as you type; they are not run. Each message starts with the name of the rule in brackets.`
// %%
viewof src_errors = sourceBox(examples.errors)
// %%
checkBox(src_errors)
// %%
md`\`jittered\` is how \`jitter\` should be written. It takes \`random\` as a parameter, so the dependency graph shows that this cell can differ between two runs.

**Try it.** In the box, change \`label: number\` to \`label: string\` in \`doubled\`. The message changes from an input type error to a type error on \`label * 2\`.`
// %%
md`## 3. Local writes: \`mut\` and \`freeze\`

Building an array or a typed array one element at a time is ordinary and fast, and rule 3 does not forbid it. \`mut(...)\` marks a value this cell made as writable. \`freeze(...)\` ends the writes and returns the plain value. Between the two, the value may not leave the cell.

\`leak\` returns a writable value and \`late\` writes after \`freeze\`. Both are rejected, so neither runs; each shows the checker's message. They are live here so that you can fix them.`
// %%
sourceListing(tutorial_module, invalidation, { from: "samples", to: "late" })
// %%
//ss: 03-mut.ts
// %%
md`**Try it.** Open \`leak\` and change \`return xs\` to \`return freeze(xs)\`. The edit is accepted, and \`leak\` turns from rejected to ok and runs.`
// %%
md`## 4. State and views

State is a state cell: a cell that returns \`state(initial)\`. Other cells use it in two ways. A parameter named \`count\` gets the current value and runs again when it changes. A parameter typed \`State<number>\` gets a handle to it. It is not a dependency: the cell does not run again when the value changes.

A write must be inside \`action(...)\`. An action reads state with \`read(count)\`, not through a value parameter, so it always sees the current value. \`increment\`, \`clear\` and \`save\` are actions. A cell cannot call them; the page does, when a button is clicked.

The view is a cell too. \`app\` returns JSX: a description of the page that is built again whenever \`count\` changes. An event attribute such as \`onClick\` must be given an action.`
// %%
sourceListing(tutorial_module, invalidation, { from: "count", to: "app" })
// %%
//ss: 04-counter.tsx
// %%
md`**Try it.** Press **add one**. Then open \`app\` and change \`onClick={increment}\` to \`onClick={count}\`. \`app\` no longer checks, because a number is not an action, and the buttons are replaced by the message. Change it back: the buttons return and the count is what it was.`
// %%
md`An action may be async. It can \`await\` a promise, such as an answer from a worker or a server, and write after it. Its writes commit together when it finishes, so a cell never sees half of them. Clicks are handled one at a time: a click made while an action is still waiting runs after it finishes.`
// %%
sourceListing(tutorial_module, invalidation, { from: "measure", to: "measureView" })
// %%
//ss: ./tutorial-async.tsx
// %%
test_async_click = {
  tests_on;
  // a copy whose source answers when this test opens the gate
  const copy = await ss_instance(tutorial_module, invalidation);
  let open;
  const gate = new Promise((r) => (open = r));
  copy._scope.get("measure").define("measure", [], () => (w) => gate.then(() => w.length));
  const view = ss.mount(await copy.value("measureView"), null);
  // waits are counted in animation frames until a condition holds; the cap only turns a hang into an error
  const frames = async (f, n = 600) => { for (let i = 0; i < n && !(await f()); i++) await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(r)); return f(); };
  try {
    if (!(await frames(() => { const q = ss.actionQueue(); return !q.inFlight && q.waiting === 0; }))) throw new Error("another action call stayed in flight");
    view.querySelector("button").click();
    const during = [ss.actionQueue().inFlight, await copy.value("wordSize")];
    open();
    const done = await frames(async () => (await copy.value("wordSize")) === 11);
    if (JSON.stringify(during) !== "[true,0]") throw new Error(`while waiting: in flight and wordSize ${JSON.stringify(during)}`);
    if (!done) throw new Error(`wordSize is ${await copy.value("wordSize")} after the answer`);
    return "the click started measureWord; it waited for the answer with wordSize 0, then wrote 11";
  } finally { open(); }
}
// %%
md`## 5. One function for every change

When state has several fields, separate actions that each change one field can leave it inconsistent. Here every action is the same line, \`store.update(s => step(s, event))\`, so one function, \`step\`, decides every transition. \`step\` is a plain function, and \`test_undo\` tests it without a page.`
// %%
reducer_view = framed(ss.mount(reducer_app, this))
// %%
sourceListing(reducer_module, invalidation)
// %%
moduleTable(reducer_module, invalidation)
// %%
md`The cells are in [@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-reducer](${linkTo("@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-reducer")}).`
// %%
md`**Try it.** Open the reducer module from the link under the table. In \`step\`, change \`- last\` to \`+ last\`. The edit is accepted, because the types still agree, and \`test_undo\` fails: its row in the table reads \`throws: undo left 6\`. The checker proves that a cell depends only on its inputs. It does not prove that the cell is right.`
// %%
md`## 6. Skipping cells that did not change

A cell that depends only on its inputs, and whose inputs are the same values as last time, would return the same value. So the notebook does not run it. The table counts how many times each cell body has run.`
// %%
model_view = framed(ss.mount(model_app, this))
// %%
sourceListing(model_module, invalidation)
// %%
variableTable(model_module, invalidation)
// %%
md`The cells are in [@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-model](${linkTo("@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-model")}).`
// %%
md`**Try it.** Press **bump** and read the **runs** column. \`doubled\` runs again, because \`count\` changed, and so do \`scale\` and the cells that read \`scale\`: \`area\`, \`areaFn\` and \`areaOf4\`. \`app\`, the view, runs again because it shows \`count\`. \`side\` and \`bump\` keep their old count; neither reads \`count\`. \`stamped\` runs again too, and always would: its **inputs** include \`ss_now\`, the clock, so it is never skipped.`
// %%
md`## 7. Running cells on other threads

A cell that touches nothing but its inputs can run anywhere. \`compile\` takes a group of cells and returns an ordinary async function; with a worker pool it runs them off the main thread. The type of that function is worked out from the cells it names.

This Mandelbrot set is computed in bands by a pool of workers. Move the slider to change the iteration count.`
// %%
workers_view = framed(ss.mount(workers_app, this))
// %%
workers_picture = {
  const canvas = this ?? htl.html`<canvas class="lite-mandel">`;
  canvas.width = workers_width;
  canvas.height = workers_height;
  const data = new ImageData(workers_width, workers_height);
  data.data.set(workers_image);
  canvas.getContext("2d").putImageData(data, 0, 0);
  return canvas;
}
// %%
workers_redraw = {
  // workers_maxIterAt runs when maxIter changes, before image is computed, so this is slider to picture.
  // At boot the time includes loading the page, so none is shown.
  workers_picture;
  const ms = (performance.now() - workers_maxIterAt.at).toFixed(0);
  return htl.html`<div class="lite-readout">${workers_maxIter} iterations, ${workers_width}×${workers_height}: ${workers_maxIterAt.boot ? "move the slider to time a redraw" : `redrawn ${ms} ms after the slider moved (${workers_bands.length} bands in ${workers_poolSize} workers, then drawn on the canvas)`}</div>`;
}
// %%
workers_maxIterAt = (workers_maxIter, { at: performance.now(), boot: this === undefined })   // hidden by the style cell
// %%
md`**Try it.** Press **measure again**. The table times the same cells on the main thread and in the pool, and checks that both produce the same bytes.`
// %%
viewof remeasure = Inputs.button("measure again")
// %%
benchmarkView = htl.html`<table class="lite-panel lite-bench">
<tr><td>workers in the pool</td><td>${benchmark.poolSize} (navigator.hardwareConcurrency ${benchmark.hardware})</td></tr>
<tr><td>bands (calls) per image</td><td>${benchmark.bands}</td></tr>
<tr><td>main thread, median of ${benchmark.runs}</td><td>${benchmark.mainMs.toFixed(0)} ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>worker pool, median of ${benchmark.runs}</td><td>${benchmark.poolMs.toFixed(0)} ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>same bytes from both</td><td>${badge(benchmark.same ? "ok" : "rejected", String(benchmark.same))}</td></tr>
</table>`
// %%
sourceListing(workers_module, invalidation)
// %%
moduleTable(workers_module, invalidation)
// %%
md`The cells are in [@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-workers](${linkTo("@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-workers")}).`
// %%
md`## 8. Mistakes in a view

JSX is checked like the rest of a cell: each attribute against the attributes its tag declares, and each event handler against the action it is given. Eleven cells, one mistake each. Ten are live cells that do not run: each shows the checker's message and can be fixed in place. The eleventh is in a box after them.`
// %%
sourceListing(tutorial_module, invalidation, { from: "n", to: "ok_all" })
