md`## 9. A game

Breakout, written with the pieces from the earlier sections. \`step(world, event)\` is the whole game, as in section 5. The world is one state cell. The view is JSX, split into cells so that the bricks are not rebuilt while only the ball moves, as in section 6.

One piece is new. A game needs a clock, and a cell cannot call an action. \`ticks\` is a generator cell. It yields the time since the last frame while the game is playing, and yields nothing once \`playing\` is false. \`onTick\` returns \`on(ticks, tick)\`, a *listener*: the notebook runs the action \`tick\` once for each value of \`ticks\`. A parameter typed \`Events<number>\` is a handle to the generator and not its latest value, so \`onTick\` does not run again on every frame.`
// %%
breakout_view = {
  // the game's own view, with its state (press play, paused, game over) drawn over the board
  const root = this ?? htl.html`<div class="lite-game"><div class="lite-game-state" aria-live="polite"></div></div>`;
  const game = ss.mount(breakout_app, root.__game ?? null);
  if (game !== root.__game) { root.__game?.remove(); root.prepend(game); root.__game = game; }
  const label = breakout_over ? (/game over/.test(breakout_status) ? "game over · press new game" : "cleared · press new game") : breakout_playing ? "" : "press play";
  const state = root.querySelector(".lite-game-state");
  if (state.textContent !== label) { state.textContent = label; state.hidden = !label; }
  // the 320×240 field is drawn at up to twice its size, to the width of the column (the CSS scales it
  // by --k; pointer offsets are in the field's own coordinates, so aiming is unchanged)
  if (!root.__resize) {
    root.__resize = new ResizeObserver(() => {
      const w = root.parentElement?.clientWidth ?? 320;
      root.style.setProperty("--k", String(Math.max(0.5, Math.min(2, w / 320))));
    });
    // the root is placed in the page after this cell returns; observe its container once it is there
    const attach = (tries) => root.parentElement ? root.__resize.observe(root.parentElement) : tries && requestAnimationFrame(() => attach(tries - 1));
    attach(600);
    // this cell runs again on every frame of play and reuses root; stop observing only once root has left the page
    invalidation.then(() => queueMicrotask(() => { if (!root.isConnected) root.__resize.disconnect(); }));
  }
  return root;
}
// %%
depGraph(breakout_module, invalidation)
// %%
sourceListing(breakout_module, invalidation)
// %%
moduleTable(breakout_module, invalidation)
// %%
md`The cells are in [@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-breakout](${linkTo("@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-breakout")}).`
// %%
md`**Try it.** Press **play** and move the pointer over the board. Then open the breakout module from the link under the table and, in \`step\`, change \`< 28\` (how far from the paddle's centre the ball still bounces) to \`< 60\`. The game keeps its score and its bricks, and the ball keeps moving, because the world is state and \`step\` is only the rule for changing it.`
// %%
md`\`test_replay\` plays 600 frames twice, with no browser and no clock, and checks that both runs end with the same score and ball position. A recorded list of events always replays to the same game, because \`step\` depends only on its arguments.`
// %%
md`## 10. Working with ordinary cells

A SoundScript cell can read the other cells of its module: Observable JavaScript cells, raw cells and imports. This section has two Observable JavaScript cells, a slider and a list.`
// %%
viewof shop_price = Inputs.range([0, 10], { step: 0.5, value: 3, label: "price" })
// %%
shop_basket = [{ item: "tea", qty: 2 }, { item: "cake", qty: 1 }]
// %%
md`The SoundScript cells that read them. A parameter that names a cell in another language must have a type. Each value that arrives is checked against that type and handed over as a frozen copy, so the SoundScript side holds what the type says and nothing the other cell can change later. If a value does not match, the reading cell fails, and its error names the place, such as \`rows[1].price: expected number, found string "12"\`. Cells that do not depend on it keep running.

\`cheaper\` takes the slider as \`State<number>\`. Inside an action it can write to it: the slider moves, and every cell that reads \`shop_price\`, in either language, runs again. Moving the slider by hand counts as an action call.`
// %%
sourceListing(tutorial_module, invalidation, { from: "type Line", to: "shopView" })
// %%
//ss: ./tutorial-ordinary.tsx
// %%
md`A parameter that reads an Observable JavaScript or raw cell and has no type is \`[boundary-unannotated]\`. A type the check cannot apply to a value, such as a function or a \`Promise\`, is \`[boundary-type]\`. \`State\` on a cell with no \`viewof\` is \`[boundary-state]\`, unless the cell is a SoundScript state cell imported from another module.`
// %%
test_shop_total = {
  const el = await tutorial_module.value("viewof shop_price");
  const total = await tutorial_module.value("shopTotal");
  if (total !== 3 * el.value) throw new Error(`shopTotal is ${total} at price ${el.value}`);
  return `shopTotal ${total} at price ${el.value}`;
}
// %%
md`## How it works

What the notebook does with a SoundScript cell, one mechanism per cell. None of this is part of the checked language; it is the compiler, the standard library and the editor hook in \`@tomlarkworthy/soundscript\`, which has its own tab.`
// %%
md`### What a cell compiles to

The compiler turns each cell, one at a time, into one *variable* of the notebook's Observable runtime: the object that holds a value and recomputes when an input changes. Section 6's table lists every variable of its module. A plain cell's variable has the cell's name, and its inputs are \`ss\` (the standard library) and its parameter names.

A plain cell is *memoised*: when every input is the same value (\`Object.is\`) as on its last run, the body does not run and the variable keeps its last value. Observable still recomputes the readers, which then hit their own memo. This is sound because the checker accepts only cells that are functions of their inputs and never change a value they did not make. In ordinary Observable a cell can change an object in place and keep its identity, and memoising would hide the change.

Three kinds of cell are not memoised. A cell the checker rejects, such as \`leak\` or section 8's mistakes, does not run at all: its variable raises the checker's message. A cell that takes \`now\`, \`random\`, \`fetch\` or \`log\` can differ between two runs on the same inputs. A cell that reads a generator's values may be lent the same buffer, changed between yields (\`yield borrow(buf)\`).

Whether a cell is memoised depends on the other cells of its module, so a stored edit compiles again any other cell whose form it changes.`
// %%
md`### A state cell is two variables

\`count\` in section 4 is two runtime variables, as an Observable \`viewof\` is: \`viewof count\` holds the handle and \`count\` holds the current value, which yields again after every committed write. A parameter \`count: State<number>\` is an input on \`viewof count\`, which does not change when the value does. So in section 6 \`doubled\` runs again on **bump** and \`bump\` does not. Writes made by one action call are committed together after it finishes. A cell that takes the \`State\` of a state cell in error is blocked.`
// %%
ss_highlightCode(md`### Capabilities are cells of the core

A capability parameter becomes an input on a variable of \`@tomlarkworthy/soundscript\`: \`now\` is \`ss_now\`, \`random\` is \`ss_random\`, \`fetch\` is \`ss_fetch\`, \`log\` is \`ss_log\` and \`pool\` is \`ss_pool\`; \`invalidation\` is the runtime's own. Section 6's \`stamped\` takes \`now\`, so its inputs list \`ss_now\`. \`compile\` is \`ss_compile\`, which a module that compiles defines for itself, because \`compile\` reads that module's cells:

\`\`\`
ss_compile = ss.compiler(() => ss_source(my_module))
viewof my_module = thisModule()
\`\`\``)
// %%
ss_highlightCode(md`### \`compile\` and its island

\`compile\` takes three lists of cell names:

\`\`\`
const inPool = (compile: Compile, pool: Pool) =>
  compile({
    inputs: ["y0", "y1"],                  // the function's argument: { y0, y1 }
    refs: ["width", "height", "maxIter"],  // read from the graph each time this cell runs
    outputs: ["rows"],                     // the result: Promise<{ rows }>
    options: { runtime: pool },            // a worker pool; leave it out to run on the main thread
  });
\`\`\`

The *island* is every cell upstream of the outputs, stopping at the inputs and refs; here it is \`rows\`. Island cells are ordinary cells: on the main thread \`rows\` computes one row, for the defaults \`y0 = 0\` and \`y1 = 1\`.

**The type** is computed from the cell graph. \`inPool\` is \`(args: { y0: number; y1: number }) => Promise<{ rows: Uint8ClampedArray }>\` (its type column in section 7), and \`image\` uses it without an annotation. With \`C\` the module's map from cell name to value type, it is \`(args: Pick<C, Inputs>) => Promise<Pick<C, Outputs>>\`. TypeScript cannot check that without \`C\`, so the checker types the call itself, and the TypeScript-based reference checker cannot cross-check it. The names must be literal strings; anything else is \`unsupported\`.

**The reactive link.** Each name in \`refs\` is a hidden input of the cell that calls \`compile\`, after its parameters; section 6's \`areaFn\` takes \`scale\` this way. Moving section 7's slider changes \`maxIter\`, \`inPool\` runs again and returns a new function with the new value inside it, and \`image\` recomputes because its input changed. Work still queued for the old function is dropped.

**The rules**, each a rejection the checker's fixtures exercise: a name must be a cell; an island cell must be accepted, synchronous, and take no capability and no state cell (put the state cell in \`refs\`); inputs, refs and outputs must survive \`structuredClone\` (no functions, refs or actions); \`options.runtime\` must be a \`Pool\`; and a call must match the computed argument type. \`compile\` refuses to run unless the checker accepts the calling cell and every island cell (\`test_compile_refuses_unchecked\`). That refusal is the only guard: the island's code runs in the worker unsandboxed, and keeping it to its inputs is the checker's job.

Measured in headless Chromium over ten page loads on 2026-11-14: section 7's 640×420 image takes about 64 ms on the main thread and about 10 ms on a pool of 8 workers, and both give the same bytes (\`test_compile_pool_matches_main\`).`)
// %%
md`### Listeners

A listener cell returns \`on(events, action)\`. While that value is the cell's value, the notebook runs the action once for each value the events cell yields, in order. It asks for the next value only after the action has run, so nothing is skipped or delivered twice. The listener ends when the cell runs again, is deleted, or its module is disposed. Starting and stopping the events themselves is ordinary dataflow: \`ticks\` reads \`playing\`, and the runtime restarts a generator cell when an input changes.`
// %%
md`### The renderer

Each JSX node becomes an \`h(tag, props, ...children)\` call, and \`h\` returns a frozen \`{tag, props, children}\` value. The page shows a cell whose type is \`Elem\` by handing that value to the renderer (\`ss.mount\`, called from the core's \`ss_language.inspect\`), 75 lines outside the checked language. The renderer creates the DOM once, then patches it in place each time \`app\` produces a new value: the same tag at the same position reuses the node, an element that is the same object as last time is skipped, and children that all carry \`key\` are matched by key. It calls the actions when DOM events arrive, decoding each event into data (\`PointerData\` from a \`PointerEvent\`, a string from an \`input\`); that decoding is trusted code.

Section 9 is built on this. \`bricks\`, \`ball\` and \`paddle\` each select one part of the world, and \`bricksView\`, \`paddleView\` and \`ballView\` draw one part each. \`step\` returns the same \`bricks\` array when nothing was hit, so \`bricksView\`'s body does not run and \`app\` gets the same element object as before, and the 24 bricks cost one comparison per frame. A broken brick removes one node. \`test_breakout_memo\` measures this on a copy of the game; on 2026-11-15 in headless Chromium, 92 animation frames gave 90 changes of the world; \`ballView\` ran 91 times, \`bricksView\` once for each brick that broke (4), \`paddleView\` not at all with no pointer moving, \`ticks\` twice (play, then pause) and \`onTick\` not at all. The DOM saw 90 \`style\` writes, 7 text changes and 4 nodes removed.

The board is \`div\`s positioned by \`style\` strings, since the checked JSX tags have no SVG or canvas drawing. A transparent \`canvas\` on top supplies \`onPointerMove\`.`
// %%
md`### How the typed source is kept

A compiled variable's definition is ordinary JavaScript. Its first comment is the marker \`/*{lang:"ss"}*/\`, the types are kept as \`/*ss:…*/\` comments, JSX as \`/*ss<…*/\` beside its lowered \`h(...)\` calls, and the text around the function (the comments before it, \`const name = \`) in a \`/*ss^{…}*/\` header. So the typed source is recovered from the definition alone, and a definition survives anything that keeps a function's text, such as saving the notebook. \`test_ss_roundtrip\` checks every live variable: decompile, compile again, and compare the text.`
// %%
md`### Editing
Shift-Enter always stores the edit. The module is checked again, and each cell is then ok, rejected (it breaks a rule) or blocked (it depends on a cell that is not ok). Only ok cells run. A rejected or blocked cell keeps its source, shows why it is not running, and is saved that way. A state cell that stays ok keeps its value while other cells are broken and repaired. A new cell takes the language of the cell it was added after; the picker in the editor's header shows it and changes it. The language registers on the \`cell_languages\` plugin point of this notebook's copy of \`@tomlarkworthy/cell-map\`, which the editor and the visualizer read.`
// %%
md`### Why four sections are modules

A module is one namespace, and the example sources are shared with the checker's test fixtures, so they keep their names; \`app\` is in four of them. Two ways to make every section live were possible: rename the cells of each section (\`reducer_step\`, \`game_step\`), which changes the text the reader edits and the fixtures check, or give each colliding section its own module. This notebook does the second. The cost: sections 5, 6, 7 and 9 are edited in their own modules, opened by the link under each table, and this page imports each section's \`app\` under another name (\`reducer_app\`, \`breakout_app\`).

Section 2's mistakes stay in a box because \`width\` and \`area\` are section 1's names; \`bad_free_component\` stays in a box because, live, it would name a variable that does not exist.`
// %%
md`## What is guaranteed

For a cell the checker accepts: it reads only its parameters and the allowed built-ins; while it runs it writes only values it made; a state cell changes only when the page calls an action; and every effect is a named parameter. A cell with no capability parameter returns the same value for the same inputs. A cell the checker rejects does not run. It shows the checker's message, and so does every cell that depends on it, until it is repaired. The stored state is decided by the ordered list of action calls and their arguments. It does not depend on which calls share a commit or on when cells run again.

Not guaranteed:

- The checker is a prototype. On 2026-11-16 a reference checker built on TypeScript's compiler could judge 149 of the specification's 159 rejected sources and gave the stated rule for 116. The checker here gives the stated rule for all 159 ("How good is the checker").
- A cell may take \`now\` or \`random\` and be accepted. It is then not repeatable, and its signature shows it.
- An events cell that reads state restarts when it sees the state change. Two changes inside one commit are seen as one, so a clock paused and resumed in the same commit does not restart.`
// %%
md`## Language reference

One entry per construct, as the checker checks it on 2026-11-14. The rules come from the language's v0 rule list; entries marked *lite* are additions made in this checker and are not in that list. The bracketed name is the rule a violation reports, as in the cell tables.`
// %%
md`### Cells

A source file holds cells and type declarations, nothing else (\`[module-level]\`).

| form | meaning |
|---|---|
| \`const x = (a, b) => …\` | a cell \`x\` with inputs \`a\` and \`b\`; \`let\`, a \`function\` expression and \`function x(a, b) {…}\` are the same |
| \`const x = async (a) => …\` | value type \`T\` for a body returning \`Promise<T>\` |
| \`const x = function* (a) {…}\` | a generator cell; value type \`Y\` for \`Generator<Y>\`; generators cannot be arrows |
| \`const x = (): StateCell<T> => state(v)\` | a state cell: value type \`T\` (see State) |
| \`const f = () => (n: number) => n * 2\` | a cell whose value is a function: two arrows, the outer one lists the inputs |
| \`type T = …\`, \`interface I {…}\`, \`import type\` | allowed at top level, not cells |

A top-level \`const\` or \`let\` that is not a function is \`[cell-not-function]\`; a second cell with the name of another is \`[duplicate-cell]\` and does not run, and the first keeps the name. Two cells that read each other are \`[cycle]\`. A cell named \`test_*\` is a test and passes if it does not throw.`
// %%
md`### Inputs

Each parameter of a cell names what it reads. What it receives depends on the name and the annotation:

| parameter | receives | a dependency on the value? |
|---|---|---|
| \`a\` or \`a: T\`, naming a cell | the cell's current value; an annotation must accept it (\`[inject-type]\`) | yes |
| \`count: State<T>\`, naming a state cell | the handle, with \`set\` and \`update\` and no read (\`[inject-state]\` if not a state cell of \`T\`) | no |
| \`ticks: Events<T>\`, naming a sync generator cell (*lite*) | a handle for \`on(...)\` (\`[inject-events]\` if not a generator yielding \`T\`) | no |
| a capability name (next entry) | the host's object for that effect | no |
| \`x: T\`, naming a cell in another language (section 10) | a frozen copy of its value, checked against \`T\` when it arrives (\`[boundary-unannotated]\` with no \`T\`, \`[boundary-type]\` if \`T\` cannot be checked) | yes |
| \`x: State<T>\`, naming an Observable JavaScript \`viewof x\` | the view as a handle: \`set\` and \`update\` write the control. A state cell of another module is taken the same way, imported as \`viewof x\`. (\`[boundary-state]\` if there is no \`viewof x\`) | no |
| \`x: State<T>\`, naming a SoundScript state cell imported from another module | the state cell in the module it is imported from | no |

Once any cell takes a generator as \`Events\`, a parameter reading it without \`Events\` is \`[inject-events]\`, because it would receive the handle. No cell may take a listener cell (\`[listener-placement]\`). A name that is another variable of the module, in any language, is read through the checked boundary (section 10). Any other name is \`[inject-unknown]\`; a destructuring pattern is \`[inject-pattern]\`. A body may not use a cell's name without taking it as a parameter (\`[cell-by-name]\`).

Types: a parameter needs a type from an annotation or from its context (\`[untyped-parameter]\`); a type name that is not declared is \`[unknown-type]\`. Most other mismatches, such as a \`string\` where a \`number\` is wanted, are \`[type]\`; a call with the wrong number of arguments is \`[arity]\`.`
// %%
md`### Capabilities

Reserved parameter names; the host supplies one per cell that asks.

| name | type | effect |
|---|---|---|
| \`now\` | \`Clock = () => number\` | the time in ms |
| \`random\` | \`Random = () => number\` | a random number |
| \`fetch\` | \`Fetch\` | network: \`(url, init?) => Promise<FetchResponse>\` |
| \`log\` | \`Log = (...args) => void\` | a line in the program's log |
| \`invalidation\` | \`Promise<void>\` | resolves when this run of the cell is replaced |
| \`compile\` | \`Compile\` (*lite*) | turns a group of cells into a function (section 7) |
| \`pool\` | \`Pool\` (*lite*), \`{ size: number }\` | the page's worker pool, for \`compile\`'s \`runtime\` |

A cell that takes none of these is deterministic.`
// %%
md`### Names a body may use

Its parameters, its own locals, the prelude (\`state\`, \`action\`, \`mut\`, \`freeze\`, \`borrow\`, \`snapshot\`, \`equals\`, and \`on\` in *lite*), and these intrinsics: \`Object Array Math JSON Number String Boolean BigInt Symbol Map Set Error TypeError RangeError RegExp Promise\`, the typed arrays, \`ArrayBuffer DataView TextEncoder TextDecoder Infinity NaN undefined isNaN isFinite parseInt parseFloat encodeURIComponent decodeURIComponent encodeURI decodeURI atob btoa structuredClone URL URLSearchParams\`. The v0 rules allow all of these, but the checker has no declarations yet for \`Symbol\`, \`DataView\`, \`TextEncoder\` and \`TextDecoder\`: using one is *unsupported*, and \`new\` on one is \`[new-non-intrinsic]\`; \`new Promise\` is also unsupported. Any other name is \`[free-name]\`. Some members of allowed objects are banned, \`Math.random\` among them (\`[banned-member]\`); a member a declared type does not have is \`[no-member]\`, and any member of an intrinsic that the checker's declarations leave out is reported *unsupported*, not ok.

Not available at all: \`Date\`, \`console\`, \`window\`, \`document\`, \`globalThis\`, \`fetch\` (use the capability), timers, \`eval\`, \`Function\`.`
// %%
md`### Immutable values

Values are read-only. Rejected:

| construct | rule |
|---|---|
| \`a.b = …\`, \`a[i] = …\`, \`++\`/\`--\`/\`+=\` on a member | \`[member-write]\` |
| \`push pop shift unshift splice sort reverse fill copyWithin set add delete clear\` on an array, map, set or typed array | \`[mutating-method]\` |
| \`delete a.b\` (allowed on a \`Mut\`) | \`[no-delete]\` |
| \`Object.assign(existing, …)\`; \`Object.assign({}, …)\` is reported unsupported, use a spread | \`[object-assign]\` |
| \`===\`, \`!==\`, \`==\`, \`!=\` on two non-primitive values; use \`equals(a, b)\` | \`[strict-eq-object]\` |
| reassigning a \`const\` | \`[const-assign]\` |
| \`var\`, \`class\`, \`this\`, \`arguments\` | \`[no-var]\`, \`[no-class]\`, \`[no-this]\`, \`[no-arguments]\` |
| \`new\` on anything but an allowed intrinsic | \`[new-non-intrinsic]\` |
| \`any\`, \`as\` (except \`as const\`), \`x!\`, \`@ts-ignore\`, \`enum\`, \`namespace\`, \`import()\` | \`[no-any]\` (inside a cell; not checked in a top-level \`type\`), \`[no-cast]\`, \`[no-non-null]\`, \`[no-ts-ignore]\`, \`[no-enum]\`, \`[no-namespace]\`, \`[no-dynamic-import]\` |

Allowed: local \`let\` and reassigning it, loops, \`throw\`, \`try\`/\`catch\`. A \`let\` may be assigned from a nested function only if that function is a local helper called directly and never passed on (\`[let-capture]\`). Reading a property or index of a value that may be \`undefined\` is \`[possibly-undefined]\`. On this page an index read \`a[i]\` has type \`T | undefined\`, so \`xs[0] * 2\` is rejected; narrow it or write \`xs[0] ?? 0\`. (The comparison in "How good is the checker" uses \`T\`, as TypeScript does.)

Not modelled, reported *unsupported*: \`switch\`, \`for…in\`, \`instanceof\`, \`??=\` \`&&=\` \`||=\`, destructuring assignment (\`[a, b] = [b, a]\`), tagged templates, the comma operator, \`satisfies\`, a \`type\` declared inside a body, \`yield*\`, \`for await\`, and, in a function holding a \`Mut\`, \`try/finally\` and labelled \`break\`.`
// %%
md`### Local mutation: \`mut\`, \`freeze\`

\`const out = mut(new Float32Array(n))\` gives a \`Mut<Float32Array>\` that the cell may write; \`freeze(out)\` ends the writing and gives the plain value (section 3). The checker tracks the \`Mut\` binding, not the object.

| rule | |
|---|---|
| \`mut(e)\` only as the initializer of a local \`const\` | \`[mut-bind]\` |
| \`e\` must be fresh: an array or object literal, a spread copy, \`new\` on an intrinsic, or an intrinsic result marked fresh | \`[mut-not-fresh]\` |
| a \`Mut\` may not be returned, yielded, aliased, put in a literal, or captured by an escaping function | \`[mut-escape]\`, \`[mut-capture]\` |
| no use after \`freeze\` | \`[mut-after-freeze]\` |
| writes only at the first level (\`out[i] = …\`, not \`out.a.b = …\`) | \`[mut-deep-write]\` |
| a view (\`subarray\`) of a \`Mut\` buffer may only be passed straight to a non-retaining intrinsic, not bound to a name | \`[mut-view]\` |
| only a \`Mut\` made by this function may be frozen, not a \`Mut\` parameter | \`[mut-freeze-param]\` |

A helper may take a \`Mut<T>\` parameter and write it. A local helper that is only called may also capture one (S4.5 in the specification). The reference checker rejects every capture.`
// %%
md`### Lending a buffer: \`borrow\`, \`snapshot\`

A generator cell may keep a \`Mut\` across yields and \`yield borrow(buf)\`; readers get a \`Borrowed<T>\`, read-only, valid during their own synchronous run. \`borrow\` appears only as the operand of \`yield\` (\`[borrow-placement]\`). A reader may not keep a \`Borrowed\` across \`await\` (\`[borrowed-await]\`), return it, store it or capture it in a nested function such as a \`map\` callback (\`[borrowed-escape]\`); \`snapshot(b)\` copies it to a plain \`T\`. A cell that reads a generator is not memoised, because the lent buffer changes in place between yields.`
// %%
md`### State: \`state\`, \`action\`

| construct | meaning |
|---|---|
| \`const count = (): StateCell<number> => state(0)\` | a state cell; \`state(...)\` only as the returned value of a sync, non-generator cell (\`[state-placement]\`); its content may not contain a \`State\` or \`StateCell\`, in a record, an array or a union (\`[state-in-state]\`) |
| \`count\` or \`count: number\` as a parameter | the current value; the reader runs again after each committed write |
| \`count: State<number>\` as a parameter | the handle: \`set(v)\`, \`update(f)\`, no read |
| \`action((by: number) => count.update((c) => c + by))\` | an \`Action<[number]>\`; writes are legal only lexically inside \`action(...)\` (\`[state-write]\`) |
| \`read(count)\` inside an action, with \`count: State<number>\` | the value as of this point in the call order: the stored value plus the writes made earlier in the same commit; legal only inside an action body (\`[read-outside-action]\`) |
| \`(other: State<number>, count: number) => action(() => other.set(count + 1))\` | rejected: an action body may not use a value that depends on state, that is a state cell's or an events cell's value, \`now\`, \`random\` or \`fetch\`, or a local computed from one (\`[action-capture]\`); \`log\` is allowed. A value from the clock or the random source reaches an action as an argument, from an events cell or a view, so it is recorded with the call: Breakout's \`ticks\` takes \`now\` and yields \`dt\`, and \`onTick\` passes it to \`tick\`. |

A cell body never calls an action (\`[action-call]\`); an action may call another action inside its own body. A cell returns an action, puts it in a record, passes it to an \`Action\` parameter, a JSX event prop or \`on(...)\`; an action where something else is expected is \`[action-pass]\`. The host calls it. Writes made by one action call are committed together after it returns.`
// %%
md`### Events and listeners: \`Events\`, \`on\` (*lite*)

| construct | meaning |
|---|---|
| \`const ticks = function* (now: Clock, playing: boolean) {…}\` | a sync generator cell taken as events; writes nothing; it runs again when an input changes; an async generator cannot be taken as \`Events\` yet |
| \`ticks: Events<number>\` | a handle to the generator, not its values; the same handle across the generator's runs |
| \`const onTick = (ticks: Events<number>, tick: Action<[number]>) => on(ticks, tick)\` | a listener: \`tick\` runs once for each value of \`ticks\`; the action must accept what \`ticks\` yields (\`[type]\`) |
| \`flag ? on(a, x) : on(b, y)\` | a listener on one of two events cells |

\`on(...)\` is legal only as the whole value of a sync cell: not in a list or record, a state cell, a function's return, an argument, or a union with another value such as \`null\`, and no cell may take a listener cell (\`[listener-placement]\`). The generator is pulled only while a listener is attached, a sync one once per animation frame. If an action throws, the error is logged to the console as \`listener <cell>: action <action> threw on a value of events <events>\`, the action's writes are discarded, and the next value is still delivered. If the generator throws, the error is logged and it yields nothing more until it runs again. When two listener cells take the same events cell, each value goes to them in the order the cells appear in the module, and listeners in other modules after them, by module name; editing or re-running a listener does not change its place. Section 9.`
// %%
md`### Workers: \`compile\` (*lite*)

\`compile({ inputs: [...], refs: [...], outputs: [...], options: { runtime: pool } })\`, every list a literal array of cell names. The *island* is the cells needed to compute \`outputs\` from \`inputs\`. The value is a function of type \`(args: Pick<C, inputs>) => Promise<Pick<C, outputs>>\`, where \`C\` maps cell names to value types; it runs on the pool when \`runtime\` is given, on the page otherwise.

When the cell calling \`compile\` runs again (a ref it names changed), the old function is superseded. Its calls already sent to a worker finish and resolve with their own result; its calls still queued reject with a \`SupersededError\`. The runtime discards the value of a cell's earlier run, so a cell such as \`image\` that awaits the function never shows a picture for an old input.

| rule | |
|---|---|
| island cells accepted, synchronous, no capability, no state cell (name it in \`refs\`) | \`[compile-island]\` |
| inputs, refs and outputs survive \`structuredClone\`: no functions, state handles, actions or borrowed values | \`[compile-boundary]\` |
| \`options.runtime\` is a \`Pool\` | \`[compile-options]\` |

Each name in \`refs\` is a hidden input of the calling cell, so a change gives a new function. \`compile\` refuses to run it unless the checker accepts the calling cell and every island cell. Section 7.`
// %%
md`### Views: JSX (*lite*)

In a \`.tsx\` source, \`<tag prop={…}>children</tag>\` is a value of type \`Elem\`; spread attributes (\`{...props}\`) are unsupported. A fragment \`<>…</>\` groups children. A capitalised local function returning \`Elem\`, \`const Card = (p: { title: string }) => <div>{p.title}</div>\`, is a component used as \`<Card title="x" />\`; one that returns something else is \`[type]\`. Allowed tags: \`div span p label strong fieldset legend output button input canvas\`. Props: \`class style title id key\` on all; \`onClick disabled\` on \`button\`; \`type name value min max step checked disabled onInput onClick\` on \`input\`; \`width height onPointerDown onPointerMove onPointerUp onPointerCancel\` on \`canvas\`. A prop not declared for the tag is \`[excess-property]\` (so are \`data-*\` and \`aria-*\`); a tag not in the list, such as \`a ul li img select textarea section\`, is \`[no-member]\`. Event props take an \`Action\`: \`onClick\` an \`Action<[]>\`, \`onInput\` an \`Action<[string]>\`, pointer events an \`Action<[PointerData]>\` with \`x y t id buttons\`. Children are elements, strings, numbers, booleans, \`null\`/\`undefined\`, or arrays of elements. The full declarations are in the first appendix; section 8 shows each rejection.`
// %%
md`## How good is the checker

Measured against a reference checker that runs TypeScript's own compiler over the same rules. The first two rows are from 2026-11-16, the others from 2026-11-14. The fixtures and the three ports (lopecode notebooks rewritten in SoundScript) are not in this notebook.

\`\`\`
remaining fixtures      13 of 13 same verdict and reason
spec rejected sources   159; the reference can judge 149 and gives the stated rule for 116
                        (10 name cells in other languages; for the other 33 the reference
                         lacks the rule or reports a different or an extra diagnostic)
three ports             144 + 25 + 38 cells, all ok   (index reads typed T, as TypeScript does)
                        6 + 3 + 3 rejected            (index reads typed T | undefined)
173 mutants of ports    0 accepted that the reference rejects
                        2 rejected that the reference accepts (a number made a string, then used as a number)
                        12 unsupported (renamed intrinsic members)
fixture and port cells  5 of 503 accepted that the reference rejects: three helpers capturing a Mut
                        (allowed deliberately), and one cell each for mut-escape and mut-after-freeze
known slips             12 of 13 rejected
check time, CLI         53-63 ms lite, 282-331 ms the reference (whole process, median of 5)
\`\`\`

In this page:`
// %%
sizes = htl.html`<table class="lite-panel">
<tr><td>parser attachment loaded by this page (acorn with its TypeScript plugin); the checker, compiler, renderer and standard library are cells</td><td>${(ss_bytes.raw / 1024).toFixed(0)} KiB, ${(ss_bytes.gz / 1024).toFixed(0)} KiB gzipped</td></tr>
<tr><td>section 7's 13 cells, check time in this browser, median of 20</td><td>${checkTime.toFixed(1)} ms</td></tr>
</table>`
// %%
md`Limits that apply to everything on this page:

- No \`keyof\`, indexed access types, tuple rest or variadic inference. A cell that needs one is \`unsupported\`, never \`ok\`.
- A module here holds one source file's worth of cells. The ports also have unchecked cells and trusted foreign signatures, which the checker reads when given them; this page does not pass them.
- The renderer's event decoders (\`PointerData\` from a \`PointerEvent\`, a string from an \`input\`) are trusted code.
- A \`.tsx\` source has no TypeScript cross-check: the reference checker does not read JSX, so the JSX and \`compile\` rules are checked by this checker alone.`
// %%
md`## Not built

Discussed on 2026-11-14; none of these exists in the checker or in this page.

- **Effects as tagged libraries.** \`state\`, \`action\`, \`Events\` and \`on\` are special cases inside the checker. The proposal moves them into declaration files whose tags (\`@write\`, \`@handle\`, \`@cell\`) say what each function may do, so a new kind of effect needs a library rather than a checker change.
- **Actors**, as a library on those tags: an inbox cell, a post action, and the inbox's messages as \`Events<M>\`. A \`mailbox\` primitive in the checker was proposed and rejected.
- **Label, do not reject.** Accept \`Math.random\` and label the cell *unsound*, and every cell that takes a capability from it, instead of rejecting it; a \`sound(...)\` marker would turn the label back into an error where it matters.
- **Typed reflection.** A typed \`Variable\` and \`Module<C>\`, so a cell that defines or redefines other cells is checked and a redefinition rechecks the cells downstream. The checker lacks \`keyof\`, bounds and indexed access, which this needs.
- **Results kept across a reload.** A deterministic cell's result stored in the saved file and reused when its inputs are unchanged.
- **Export to ObservableHQ.** The compiled cells are ordinary JavaScript, but the marker comments and the editor hook exist only in this notebook.
- **\`now\` and \`random\` per cell.** Every cell that takes \`now\` gets the core's one \`ss_now\`, and every cell that takes \`random\` the one \`ss_random\`; a recorded or seeded source for each cell is not built.`
// %%
md`## Tests

The tests are off until **run the tests** is on, so reading the page costs nothing for them. Each test of this module takes \`tests_on\`, which stays pending until then. The Breakout tests play a copy of section 9's cells (\`ss_instance\`), not the game on this page, and \`test_ss_counter\` presses **add one** and **clear** on a copy of section 4's counter, so the counter on the page keeps its count. The section modules' own tests (\`test_undo\`, \`test_replay\`) are cheap and always run. The core's tests are in the core tab, behind its own switch.`
// %%
viewof run_tests = {
  const toggle = Inputs.toggle({ label: "run the tests" });
  toggle.classList.add("lite-tests-toggle");
  return toggle;
}
// %%
tests_on = run_tests ? true : invalidation
// %%
testsView = testTable([["tutorial", tutorial_module], ["reducer", reducer_module], ["model", model_module], ["workers", workers_module], ["breakout", breakout_module]], invalidation)
// %%
test_examples_check_as_expected = {
  tests_on;
  const want = { cells: [7, 0], errors: [8, 5], mut: [4, 2], counter: [6, 0], reducer: [10, 0], model: [10, 0], workers: [13, 0], rejectJsx: [17, 11], game: [21, 0] };
  const got = {};
  for (const [k, [n, rejected]] of Object.entries(want)) {
    const r = ss.checkSources({ cells: examples[k], tsx: k !== "cells" && k !== "errors" && k !== "mut" });
    const bad = r.cells.filter((c) => c.status === "rejected").length;
    got[k] = [r.cells.length, bad];
    if (r.cells.length !== n || bad !== rejected) throw new Error(`${k}: ${r.cells.length} cells, ${bad} rejected; want ${n}, ${rejected}`);
  }
  return got;
}
// %%
test_compile_pool_matches_main = {
  tests_on;
  const image = await workers_image;
  const main = await workers_onMain({ y0: 0, y1: workers_height });
  if (main.rows.length !== image.length || !main.rows.every((v, i) => v === image[i])) throw new Error("pool and main thread differ");
  return `${image.length} bytes from ${workers_bands.length} pool calls equal one main-thread call`;
}
// %%
test_compile_refuses_unchecked = {
  tests_on;
  // the workers example with one island cell made to read Math.random, which the checker rejects
  const source = examples.workers.replace("out[at + 3] = 255;", "out[at + 3] = Math.random();");
  if (source === examples.workers) throw new Error("rows no longer writes out[at + 3] = 255; update this test");
  const c = ss.compiler(() => source);
  try {
    c.for("inPool", { width: 8, height: 8, maxIter: 10 })({ inputs: ["y0", "y1"], refs: ["width", "height", "maxIter"], outputs: ["rows"] });
  } catch (e) {
    if (!/^compile refused: (\w+, )*rows not ok/.test(e.message)) throw e;
    return e.message;
  }
  throw new Error("compile ran an island with a rejected cell");
}
// %%
quietThen = async (f) => {
  // f run once no action call is in flight or waiting, so an action it calls is a call of its own;
  // the frame cap only turns a hung call into an error
  const quiet = () => { const q = ss.actionQueue(); return !q.inFlight && q.waiting === 0; };
  for (let i = 0; i < 600 && !quiet(); i++) await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(r));
  if (!quiet()) throw new Error("an action call stayed in flight for 600 frames");
  return f();
}
// %%
test_breakout_listener = {
  tests_on;
  // its own copy of section 9's cells: the game on the page is not played or reset
  const game = await ss_instance(breakout_module, invalidation);
  // waits are counted in animation frames until a condition holds; the cap only turns a hung page into an error
  const frames = async (f, n = 600) => { for (let i = 0; i < n && !f(); i++) await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(r)); return f(); };
  const get = (n) => game._scope.get(n)._value;
  const runs = (n) => ss.evals(game._scope.get(n)._definition);
  for (const n of ["reset", "toggle", "world", "bricks", "onTick"]) await game.value(n);
  // the clock of this copy only: other tests on the page pull generators of their own
  const w0 = get("world"), on0 = runs("onTick"), t0 = runs("ticks"), h0 = get("ticks");
  await quietThen(() => get("toggle")());
  let changes = 0, last = w0;
  await frames(() => { const w = get("world"); if (w !== last) { changes++; last = w; } return changes >= 10; });
  const playing = ss.pulling([h0]), w1 = get("world");
  await quietThen(() => get("toggle")());
  await frames(() => ss.pulling([h0]) === 0, 60);
  const w2 = get("world"), paused = ss.pulling([h0]);
  await frames(() => false, 10);
  const w3 = get("world");
  const onTick = runs("onTick") - on0, ticks = runs("ticks") - t0;
  if (playing !== 1 || w1.ball.y === w0.ball.y) throw new Error(`playing: ${playing} clocks pulled, ball ${w0.ball.y} -> ${w1.ball.y}; a hidden tab gets no animation frames`);
  if (paused !== 0 || w3 !== w2) throw new Error(`paused: ${paused} clocks pulled, the world ${w3 === w2 ? "kept" : "changed"}`);
  if (onTick !== 0 || ticks !== 2 || get("ticks") !== h0) throw new Error(`onTick ran ${onTick} times, ticks ${ticks} times, same handle ${get("ticks") === h0}`);
  return `play: the clock runs and the ball moved ${w0.ball.y} -> ${w1.ball.y.toFixed(1)}; pause: it stops and the world stays the same object; ticks ran twice, onTick not at all`;
}
// %%
test_breakout_memo = {
  test_breakout_listener; // both count clocks pulled, so they run one after the other
  const game = await ss_instance(breakout_module, invalidation);
  const get = (n) => game._scope.get(n)._value;
  for (const n of ["reset", "toggle", "world", "bricks", "app"]) await game.value(n);
  const runs = (n) => ss.evals(game._scope.get(n)._definition);
  // the copy's app drawn into a node of its own, as breakout_view draws the page's
  let view = null;
  const drawn = new Promise((res) => invalidation.then(observe(game._scope.get("app"), { fulfilled(a) { view = ss.mount(a, view); res(); } })));
  await drawn;
  const muts = {};
  const mo = new MutationObserver((rs) => { for (const r of rs) { const k = r.type === "attributes" ? "attr " + r.attributeName : r.type; muts[k] = (muts[k] ?? 0) + (r.type === "childList" ? r.addedNodes.length + r.removedNodes.length : 1); } });
  const names = ["ballView", "bricksView", "paddleView", "status", "app", "ticks", "onTick"];
  mo.observe(view, { subtree: true, childList: true, attributes: true, characterData: true });
  const n0 = Object.fromEntries(names.map((n) => [n, runs(n)]));
  const b0 = get("bricks").length;
  // played for a number of delivered ticks and until a brick breaks, not for a time, so the counts do
  // not depend on the frame rate (step clamps a tick to at most 50 ms of game time)
  const TICKS = 90;
  let frames = 0, changes = 0, last = get("world");
  await quietThen(() => get("toggle")());
  // the frame cap only turns a hung page into an error
  const CAP = 3000;
  await new Promise((res) => { const f = () => { frames++; const w = get("world"); if (w !== last) { changes++; last = w; } if ((changes < TICKS || get("bricks").length === b0) && frames < CAP) requestAnimationFrame(f); else res(); }; requestAnimationFrame(f); });
  const h0 = get("ticks");
  await quietThen(() => get("toggle")());
  // the clock has stopped, and the mutations of the last tick have been delivered
  for (let i = 0; i < 60 && ss.pulling([h0]) > 0; i++) await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(r));
  await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(r));
  mo.disconnect();
  const ran = Object.fromEntries(names.map((n) => [n, runs(n) - n0[n]]));
  const broken = b0 - get("bricks").length;
  if (changes < TICKS) throw new Error(`only ${changes} world changes in ${frames} frames; a hidden tab gets no animation frames`);
  if (broken < 1) throw new Error("no brick broke, so the keyed path was not exercised");
  if ((muts["attr style"] ?? 0) > ran.ballView + ran.paddleView + 2) throw new Error(`${muts["attr style"]} style writes for ${ran.ballView + ran.paddleView} ball and paddle draws`);
  if (ran.bricksView > broken) throw new Error(`bricksView ran ${ran.bricksView} times for ${broken} broken bricks`);
  if (ran.paddleView > 0) throw new Error(`paddleView ran ${ran.paddleView} times with no pointer move`);
  if (ran.onTick > 0 || ran.ticks > 2) throw new Error(`onTick ran ${ran.onTick} times and ticks ${ran.ticks} times for one play and one pause`);
  if ((muts.childList ?? 0) > broken) throw new Error(`${muts.childList} nodes added or removed for ${broken} broken bricks`);
  return { frames, worldChanges: changes, broken, runs: ran, domMutations: muts };
}
